From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Arguing Easily Resolvable Issues

Many issues Extropians argue are intrinsically difficult to resolve. Some
are philosophical issues, some are esthetic/preference issues, and some are
just not answerable questions.

Other issues are "common knowledge" issues, such as the recent "Does the
U.S. ban competitors to the Post Office?" flap. 

Duncan Frissell was the first to cite the statutes passed in the 1840s. (By
the way, a major theme of one of Thomas Pynchon's novels, "The Crying of
Lot 49," deals with an outlaw mail delivery service, called "W.A.S.T.E.,"
which originated in the 1850s when private postal services were outlawed.
My .sig has contained my "W.A.S.T.E." address for years. 

Several of us also pointed to the existence of many articles and books
written about "ending the Postal Service monopoly." And we cited the
limitations placed on FedEx, Airborne, and UPS. Perry M. even bet $1000
that Jeff Fabijanic's interpretation was incorrect.

Now the debate is being shifted by Jeff as to whether Perry accepted the
bet, or the counteroffer, or whatever. A complete waste of time, especially
since everyone seems to agree that the issue got resolved when Duncan
Frissell posted a summary of legal rulings.

But the "debate" didn't end, as Jeff F. continued to call for Perry to
answer his challenges, and talked about the philosophical impossibility of
ever proving a negative (something about not being able to prove that blue
rhinoceri don't exist....).

Folks, this is a nonsensical waste of our time! I have already elected to
begin using the new list software (to ::exclude either a thread, or a
user...I'm not saying :-} ).

Dave Krieger is right that most of us do not carry our reference libraries
around with us, or even have them well-organized enough at home to find
things instantly. But resources exist. For example, a few days ago I
searched on the University of California's library system, all 9 campuses,
for references on this.

I'll describe how anybody with Internet access (telnet) can do it.

"telnet melvyl.ucop.edu"

specify terminal type (e.g., "vt100")

"catalog" (gives you the huge Melvyl catalog)

"f tw postal monopoly" (find all books with "postal" and "monopoly" in the
title)

Here's what I found, edited to show the most relevant books:

