I'm sure several dozen other large university libraries are also freely
available via telnet. Looking through the books available on some topic is
often a quick and easy way to get the lay of the land, to find out what the
main scholarly issues are. In this case, it was apparent that a postal
monopoly of some sort does exist, as most of us said. The exact form, the
taxes levied on competitors (if that's done), the rates set, etc., await
further research. These books would be where I'd start, not reading the
case law itself.

These books, some of them discussed for years in the pages of
"Laissez-Faire Books Catalog," "Reason," "Liberty," etc., are just some of
the strong reasons we have for rejecting Jeff Fabrinac's claims that we
have not proved there is a postal monopoly, that we cannot prove there is
not some law allowing competition (or whatever he meant by his point about
proving negatives), and why we have so little patience with claims that we
must "prove" things in excruciating detail...all the more ironic since Jeff
Fabrinac chose not to accept Perry's bet and instead went the route of
trying to confuse the issue with nonsense about Perry's motives in offering
the bet and with philosophical mumbo jumbo about the impossibility of ever
resolving the bet. (Need I repeat what Perry has already said, that he
several times expressed willingness to negotiate exact terms, conditions,
and amounts?)

The bet with Perry is now moot. I would go so far as to say that anyone,
including Fabijanic, who continues to claim the bet is still not being
accepted by Perry (as Fabijanic has repeatedly claimed) is being
deliberately baiting and disingenuous. 

My intent is not to flame Jeff Fabijanic here, but to underscore the
stupidity of debating such issues as whether a postal monopoly exists.

My guess is that we have another Worley Abomination here. Some people just
want a place to argue, it doesn't matter about what.


-Tim May

