Date: Mon, 20 Sep 93 21:44:40 WET DST
From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Subject: LOGIC: Core Wars (was: Superrationality)

Paul Cisek () writes:
> (The net-game CoreWar is an interesting example of a fluctuating population of
> strategies.  Anyone know if it has converged to a solution?)

  No it hasn't. The game has converged to three strategies which arrange
themselves in rock-scissors-paper fashion.

1) Dwarf-like bombers. These programs simply bomb the core mod X (X is
usually chosen to cover the core quickly, but to not hit your program
until the last minute. A clever trick is to bomb with SPL (split process)
which slows your opponent down. When your program ends up bombing itself,
it switches to "core clear" which just fills the core with DAT #0s. Modern
dwarfs bomb 2 locations for every 3 instructions executed.

2) B-Field scanners. This programs _quickly_ scan the core looking in the
B-field of instructions for a non-zero value. If they find anything, they
bomb it in a few locations.
2a) Cmp-scanners. These programs scan 2 locations at the same time by
comparing two indirect areas of the core. Normally the core starts 
initalized to all DAT #0s, so doing cmp <loc_a, <loc_b will only trigger
if it finds something. if something is found, loc_a and loc_b are bombed.

3) Imp Rings/Spirals - these programs are harder to describe because
they exploit flow of executation properties. As the name suggests, they
fill the core with imps (but imps that hop more than 1 location at a time)

I have left out the category of Mice and Vampires since they were pretty
much wiped out the dwarfs.


