Date: Mon, 20 Sep 93 19:03:54 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Being w/Smart People

My hat is off to Steve Witham for introducing an origial topic:

> Here's a topic.  How do you feel about being around people who
> are smarter than you, or as smart as you and much better
> versed in some topic, or much more talented in a certain way?

I've been around a lot of bright folks in my day, and can't say it was
much different from an Extropians lunch, or Hackers Conference, or
a technical meeting at Intel. Several of my Bay Area
friends--including at least a couple now on the list--are by any
standards at the genius level and are quick-witted as anyone I've ever
met.

The Extropolooza last month certainly was filled with very bright
people, and I found it a joy. Not at all an overload, even after 12
hours of nonstop talk (and some partying).

The only really "discombobulating" experience I've ever had--at least
in recent years--was when I attended the "Crypto" conference in 1988.
Brilliant mathematicians (Goldwassser, Micali, Shamir, and several
dozen others) speaking rapidly about zero knowledge, complexity
classes, and elliptic curves.  I was in over my head, so I settled
back to just absorb what I could. (And I carefully avoided asking
questions, for reasons I'll go into below.)

> I think generally I feel more relaxed and safe,"at home"
> somehow.  I mean, contrary to what you might think from the
> way people talk sometimes.

Not sure what is meant here, but many of us feel comfortable at home,
in our nests or lairs, with our books, music, and whatnot available.

> How do you like being in a conversation where you have to
> pick up the basic ideas of a field or topic by smell, by the
> way people raise their eyebrows or laugh at certain points,
> or ask certain questions in a pointed way?  I generally
> love it.  I even like listening to conversations and
> noticing references go by that I don't get.

Usually not my cup of tea, as I like to talk (surprise!). So I usually
try to get up to speed as quickly as possible so I can join in the
conversation. Jumping in, even prematurely, is very much like the
"making estimates" point I argued recently: some call it
"bullshitting," that is, talking about something one is relatively new
to, but in reality it's a way to quickly "make estimates" and see them
confirmed or shot down (at which point one can modify positions and
try again).

This willingness to speculate, to jump in with both feet, is
well-known to be an area that many/most women are weak in, for
whatever reasons.

...
> I went through a period where I had to push myself to be
> "rude" and ask questions.  I'm still inhibited about that.
> 
> How about you?

I'll take a radically different stance here and say that more people
ought to learn to shut up and listen and not ask stupid questions. I
know, I know, we are taught to "always ask questions" and we are
further told of how great scientists often ask embarassingly basic
questions.

This questioning has its place, but too often people ask dumb
questions as a substitute for reasoning out an answer for themselves.
Like little kids, who ask about everything....or like the people in
movie theaters who turn to their neighbors and ask stupid questions
about what just happened on the screen instead of just _watching_ the
action themselves. 

At some Cyherpunks meetings, we've had to discourage "basic" questions
by people who just don't know the basics of crypto. Watching what's
being said, doing some reading, and being reasonable is the best way I
know to come up to speed in a new area.

Steve's point about observing the flow is a good one, and that's what
I try to do. That's why when I joined the list, I "observed" or lurked
for nearly a month before posting....something I think all newcomers
should do! 


--Tim May

