Date: Mon, 20 Sep 93 20:12:56 -0700
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Being w/Smart People

> [ Steve Witham comments about being around smart people ... ]

  I think it's wonderful to be around people smarter than I am (and it's
just as well, because it seems to be unavoidable...)

  When I was quite small I encountered the idea that an interesting approach
to interpersonal relations was to assume that for each person, there existed
at least one thing which that person could do better than anyone else, and
try to find out what it was.  I tried it, and found it led to fascinating
conversations and many interesting friends.  (Somewhat later I adopted
the much easier criterion of assuming that each person had at least one
thing he or she could do better than I could.)

  I was fortunate enough to go to universities that were excellent in the
fields I was interested in (CalTech and U. C. Berkeley, theoretical physics
switching slowly into experimental astrophysics), and some of my most
wonderful memories come from listening to world-class minds expound on
topics I loved, with greater knowledge and experience than I had.  My
favorite was of course Feynman, who taught the first class I ever had
that was titled "Advanced ...", but I remember watching with glee as
Jon Mathews integrated cos(ax) from x = 0 to infinity and got a sensible
answer.  Of course, these people were good teachers as well as good
minds, but I believe contact with them in the classroom prepared me for
less formal contact with other smart people later.

  I've had to switch careers once or twice, and soaking up learning from
smart people is one way to help bootstrap.  Getting someone to talk about
a topic he or she is interested in is often a good way to learn.  Humility
and honesty about ignorance and understanding seem useful -- I agree
with Tim May that stupid questions usually are not useful.  But I have
rarely encountered objections to asking "where can I read more about ____?"

  One must bear in mind that inteliigence and experience are not the
same thing.  There are many people who are not too bright but have been
doing something long enough to be walking encyclopedias of wonder; my
personal experiences include rosarians and Jane Austen fans.  Then there
are brilliant intellects with little knowledge but the ability to see
swiftly to the core of a problem.

  I suspect that the most common reason why people dislike smarter folks
is simple ego.  However, humility seems to become more bearable with
practice, mistakes are less painful the more of them you make, and loss
of pride has for me usually been well worth gains in knowledge.

                                   -- Jay Freeman,
