Date: Mon, 20 Sep 93 23:35:45 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Being w/Smart People

FutureNerd Steve Witham:

> Here's a topic.  How do you feel about being around people who
> are smarter than you, or as smart as you and much better
> versed in some topic, or much more talented in a certain way?

Pretty damn awful at first, I'll tell you!  Often, though,
they can either be intimidated into leaving or manipulated
onto my intellectual home ground...

heh.

Situations like you describe are tremendous opportunities --
and not just for the normal views of "smart" but also for
any other admired quality, such as purposeful commitment
to self-improvement, eloquence, charisma, or whatever.

The most obvious potential in such a situation is to exercise
one's learning-curve-climbing muscles* with the best text yet
invented right there.  Sometimes it is possible to trade alcohol
or other substances for tutorial conversation and glow-basking.

Being a productive participant in groups effectively discussing
detailed interesting material is a real rush.  However, I think
it's important to curb one's natural instincts when it would
keep an existing discussion from being as interesting as it
could be if one wasn't there asking dumb questions.  A good
rule of thumb is to not spout naive questions to a group unless
you think they might be sexually attracted to you.

I remember once when I was visiting a friend who worked at Fermilab,
he had a party and a number of employees, including a few theoretical
physicists, showed up.  I had a wonderful hour watching them discuss
how to solve one of those puzzles where you have to move a wooden
ball from one loop of rope to another (they got it; apparently
nobody at that house had ever solved it before).  The group
had a definite leader.

derek
*recent scientific evidence suggests that learning is not in
fact a muscular phenomenon


